A||||9||GT Bastard||Sport springs vs. Cutting them...||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||09-30-2000||06:36 AM||||I'm running Koni's and Eibachs on my car right now, and I think it handles like a dream.  A friend of mine runs springs that he cut himself and Delco Performer shocks/struts.  When he told me his springs were cut, I thought he was a complete jack-ass.  Since then, I've driven with him numerous times on and off the autocross course.  Much to my surprise, his car easily outhandles mine!  The crazy part is, he's running heavily used Bridgestone Potenza's up front, and Goodyear Affinities in back(205's.)  His car rides harsher than mine, but OH MY GOD does it take corners.  He always seems to rip-up the Vette boys at auto-x(there aren't always classes where we auto-x and he's a good driver.)  Call me crazy, but I had assumed that cutting springs caused a car to handles like crap.  That's just what everyone seems to think.  I'll stick with my smoother Koni/Eibach ride, but if I get another Fiero in addition to mine, I'll consider cutting them!  Anybody here have cut springs?  How do you like them? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||contra||09-30-2000||08:43 AM||||it can be done<BR>but to be on the safe side<BR>i dont think $300 canadian for lowering springs is out of my budget. and i'd rather buy those and feel secure that nothing bad will happen to my car i spent alot of time and money on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>personally i'd have to sit in a car with cut springs or drive it like you did to really get a true idea of what it's like. i am still unsure of cutting.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||09-30-2000||09:06 AM||||Chop-Chop, thats what I say! Cut springs work fine for me. rides and handles great. If you're worried about affecting the temper of the steel by cutting with a torch, use an abrasive wheel to cut them. They will be no more un-safe than any springs you could buy. Cut your own springs and use the money you saved to upgrade your shocks/struts or swaybars.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000003||contra||09-30-2000||10:19 AM||||rockcrawl, has there been any problems at all? i think i'm going to remove my front air dam next spring and drop my car 1 or 2 inches. sure it would look cool with the air dam, but SCRAPE SCRAPE all the time is no good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>i think my rear springs or struts are tired cause my back end sags a little. i dont think i should cut my springs, or at least not my back ones. what would your opinion be?<BR>also how much do new shocks and struts usually cost?||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||09-30-2000||11:13 AM||||chop em', that's what i say and i'll say it again!!!bzzzzzzzclink!! bzzzzzzzzzzclink!!! on the chop saw that is. i cut my springs front and back, and then i had a 4 wheel alignment after i put on the new shocks and struts. then i got new bridgestone potenza 205 55 16's all the way around. corners like a pro. everyone has their own opinion but if springs are chopped and re-mounted correctly then you should have good results.||||reg||10
Z||000005||contra||09-30-2000||11:24 AM||||well i MIGHT consider it cause like i said, i need new springs anyways, and if the chopping doesn't do any good for me, then i'll just buy lowering springs like i was originally going to.<BR>i'm sure the local spring shop around here wont charge more than $60 to chop and reinstall my springs<BR>they jacked up my jeep like crazy for under a hundred bucks which was nice.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||KitKar||09-30-2000||02:34 PM||||How much do you have to cut to get a 2 inch drop?<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Webmaster<BR>http://www.KitKar.com||||reg||1
Z||000007||tiacPaul||10-01-2000||01:21 AM||||A friend of mine who cut his springs ended up with a pile of busted motor mounts.  They aren't designed to take that mush punishment.  Maybe that's why the springs are as big as they are!  but if you like replaceing things and working on your car go for it!  ||||reg||4
Z||000008||rockcrawl||10-01-2000||08:34 AM||||Busted motor mounts due to cutting springs?! I don't think so! I don't get that one!<P>p.s. I do like working on my car.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||10
Z||000009||Sleeper||10-01-2000||12:17 PM||||Big wheels and low profile tires is the ticket if you wanna hit the turns fast, springs help but wheels and tires are the main thing.||||reg||1
